      <workList xml:id="workDesc_04dd7112">
         <work xml:id="work_idd96c48e3">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_08af3bbe">C1:010</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id81e32b33" xml:lang="de">Schreiben an die Herren Verfasser der neuen periodischen Schrift</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_04c6c148"/>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id939de4d1">
               <date isodate="1765" xml:id="date_30febad1">1765</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_idd6625df4">
               <p xml:id="idm64">The article, which is dated 17 June 1765, was a response to a review that appeared in the periodical <rend xml:id="idm65" fontstyle="italic">Samling af adskillige Skrifter</rend> (1765). At the event, celebrating the king’s birthday and <persName xml:id="idm66" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Crown Prince Christian</persName>’s confirmation, music by Scheibe was performed. One of the works was a cantata dedicated to the prince (SchW B2:423). The reviewer criticised Scheibe’s work for being an imitation of bad Italian composers, and in particular his setting of the text or the art of the recitative (‘Recitativens Kunst’) was criticised. It was around this time that Scheibe worked intensely on the various aspects of setting and representing the emotions of texts in music, especially in the recitative. He discussed the issues with <persName xml:id="idm67" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Heinrich Wilhelm von Gerstenberg</persName> in whose house Scheibe lived around this time and with <persName xml:id="idm68" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich Gottlieb Klopstock</persName> who, however, found Scheibe a somewhat obstinate person. The present, very passionate and long response deals with various aspects of performance practice including rehearsals, setting text to music, of rhetoric, and compositional framework. Scheibe also mentions two of his works (SchW B2:423, B2:428) including long analyses of especially the cantata to Crown Prince Christian though without including music examples. Scheibe also mentions his ‘Marte schernito’ (SchW B2:428) and the Italian style in which he had to compose the work. The article is not part of the periodical but appears to have been published on Scheibe's own instigation. In the following issue the editors of the journal answer Scheibe and are rather surprised that he found it worth replying to such a minor review (‘den fattige Anmærkning’). The article was reviewed in <rend xml:id="idm69" fontstyle="italic">Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek</rend> (1767) by the composer <persName xml:id="idm70" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Friedrich Agricola</persName> who mentions that he will not take part in the discussion concerning the disagreements but only reflect on Scheibe’s approach to compositional processes; yet the reviewer does seem to support Scheibe’s views.</p>
            </history>
